# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2 has "tested_judgments → rarely_new" and "rarely_new → critics_inferior"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.3 have "rarely_new"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "I have already had frequent proof of the judgments of friends, the indifferent, and the malignant and envious", "it has rarely happened that anything has been objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject" and "I have never met with a critic of my opinions who did not appear either less rigorous or less equitable than myself" are false. Is S4, "But it has rarely happened that anything has been objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless it were something far removed from the subject: so that I have never met with a single critic of my opinions who did not appear to me either less rigorous or less equitable than myself.", true then?
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2 has "verisimilitude ∧ advocates_not_judges" and "verisimilitude ∧ advocates_not_judges → schools_no_truth"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.3 have "verisimilitude" and "advocates_not_judges"; they differ when only "in disputation each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing reasons on both sides" and "those who have been long good advocates are not on that account the better judges" are true, which the second allows
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "in disputation each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing reasons on both sides" and "those who have been long good advocates are not on that account the better judges" are true and "no truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations practised in the schools" is false. Is S5, "And further, I have never observed that any truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations that are practised in the schools; for while each strives for the victory, each is much more occupied in making the best of mere verisimilitude, than in weighing the reasons on both sides of the question; and those who have been long good advocates are not afterwards on that account the better judges.", true then?
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2 has "critics_inferior ∧ schools_no_truth → no_profit"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.2 has "rarely_new → critics_inferior" and "verisimilitude ∧ advocates_not_judges → schools_no_truth" and "tested_judgments ∧ critics_inferior ∧ schools_no_truth → no_profit"; they differ when only "I do not anticipate any profit from objections to my views", "I have already had frequent proof of the judgments of friends, the indifferent, and the malignant and envious", "it has rarely happened that anything has been objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject", "I have never met with a critic of my opinions who did not appear either less rigorous or less equitable than myself", "in disputation each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing reasons on both sides" and "those who have been long good advocates are not on that account the better judges" are true, which the first allows; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2.3 has "tested_judgments ∧ rarely_new → critics_inferior" and "verisimilitude ∧ advocates_not_judges → schools_no_truth" and "critics_inferior ∧ schools_no_truth → no_profit"
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r2 P3 (S4): the sentence has no word of "I have already had frequent proof of the judgments of friends, the indifferent, and the malignant and envious": is it the sentence P3 came from?
